{
  "paper_title": "Ethics Learned at a Distance: Distributed Entry, Ethical Imprinting, and the Generation Z Question",
  "keywords": "Generation Z, workplace ethics, organizational socialization, social learning theory, imprinting, remote work",
  "contributions": [
    "C1: Recasts ethical socialization as a transmission process whose fidelity depends on observational access to ethical conduct, and introduces enacted-norm clarity and leader ethical narration to study it under distributed work (P1, P2).",
    "C2: Proposes a two-sided effect: distributed entry weakens the transmission of a unit's enacted norms whatever their ethical quality, separating a learning account from an opportunity or monitoring account of misconduct in remote work (P3).",
    "C3: Extends imprinting theory to ethical norms and locates the Generation Z question in conditions of organizational entry rather than birth cohort, as testable propositions (P4, P5)."
  ],
